Here is a structured reference list with extended notes. I focus on three relevant domains: (1) typology and diachronic grammar (classifier/gender compression), (2) theology, exegesis, and cultural binaries, and (3) the algebraization of logic. I omit your own work, as requested.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Classifiers, Gender Systems, and Typology</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Aikhenvald, Alexandra Y. (2000). Classifiers: A Typology of Noun Categorization Devices. Oxford University Press.
Comprehensive typological survey of classifier systems across languages, showing the high cardinality of early classifier inventories and their semantic bases (shape, size, animacy, etc.). Serves as a foundation for modeling  in oral/pre-literate ecologies.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Corbett, Greville G. (1991). Gender. Cambridge University Press.
Authoritative analysis of gender systems, their agreement mechanics, and diachronic tendencies. Establishes the standard framework for â€œcollapseâ€ from multiple genders to fewer categories under pressure from usage and simplification.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Kramer, Ruth (2015). The Morphosyntax of Gender. Oxford University Press.
Focuses on the interaction between syntactic structure and gender assignment, including diachronic erosion of neuter in languages like English and Romance. Useful for the late-medieval/early-modern stage of .</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Dixon, R. M. W. (1982). Where Have All the Adjectives Gone? And Other Essays in Semantics and Syntax. Mouton de Gruyter.
Includes discussion of languages with complex classifier systems, reinforcing the oral/animist stage where many semantically grounded categories are active.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="2">
<li class="break-words">Religious Binaries, Exegesis, and Narrative Framing</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Genesis Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah). Midrash, ca. 5thâ€“6th century CE.
Classical rabbinic commentary that interprets the Noah narrative with strict binary separation of sexes and attaches moral consequences (Ham, the dog). This is a prime textual locus for the rise of .</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Augustine of Hippo (354â€“430). De Genesi ad Litteram; City of God.
Augustineâ€™s allegorical and literal readings of Genesis articulate a Christianized binary of sin/grace, male/female, spirit/flesh. Strong theological driver of binary cultural fields in Late Antiquity.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Peter Brown (1988). The Body and Society: Men, Women, and Sexual Renunciation in Early Christianity. Columbia University Press.
Examines the late antique regulation of sexuality and gender, showing how theological regimes instantiated rigid binary separations (chastity vs lust, male vs female roles).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Scholem, Gershom (1946). Major Trends in Jewish Mysticism. Schocken.
Analyzes Kabbalistic traditions where gendered binaries (male/female sefirot, union/separation) become cosmological principles. Illustrates how narrative theology reinforces binary fields.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="3">
<li class="break-words">Scholasticism, Law, and Dialectical Logic</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Leff, Gordon (1967). Medieval Thought: St. Augustine to Ockham. Penguin.
Explains how scholastic disputation institutionalized binary â€œyes/noâ€ resolutions, operationalizing the principle of non-contradiction in law, theology, and pedagogy.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Kretzmann, Norman, Anthony Kenny, and Jan Pinborg, eds. (1982). The Cambridge History of Later Medieval Philosophy: From the Rediscovery of Aristotle to the Disintegration of Scholasticism, 1100â€“1600. Cambridge University Press.
Essential resource for understanding the scholastic culture of quaestiones, which habituated intellectual communities to binary resolution of contradictory theses.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Luscombe, David (1997). Medieval Thought. Oxford University Press.
A shorter survey emphasizing how scholastic method enshrined binary structures in academic discourse.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="4">
<li class="break-words">Logic, Algebraization, and the Modern Binary</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Aristotle. Organon (esp. De Interpretatione).
Original codification of the principle of non-contradiction and excluded middle. The seed form of bivalence before its cultural entrenchment.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Boole, George (1854). An Investigation of the Laws of Thought, on Which are Founded the Mathematical Theories of Logic and Probabilities. Walton &amp; Maberly.
The decisive algebraization of binary logic. Shows how centuries of bivalent practice culminated in a formal calculus on .</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Peirce, Charles Sanders (1931â€“58). Collected Papers. Harvard University Press.
Provides both extensions and reflections on Boolean logic and its embedding in algebraic structures; demonstrates the stabilization of binary logic as a mathematical object.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Kneale, William &amp; Kneale, Martha (1962). The Development of Logic. Oxford University Press.
Historical overview from Aristotle through Boole, with emphasis on the cultural and institutional embedding of bivalence.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="5">
<li class="break-words">Cross-Domain Bridges</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Jack Goody (1977). The Domestication of the Savage Mind. Cambridge University Press.
Argues that literacy and administrative rationalization reduce classificatory diversity; strong evidence for the role of  in driving  downward.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Mary Douglas (1966). Purity and Danger: An Analysis of Concepts of Pollution and Taboo. Routledge.
Classic anthropological work on binary schemas of pure/impure, order/disorder, that underlie cultural consolidation of oppositions.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Summary</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Typology (Aikhenvald, Corbett, Kramer, Dixon): Document manyâ†’triadâ†’binary transitions in classifiers/gender.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Exegesis (Genesis Rabbah, Augustine, Brown, Scholem): Show the early medieval rise of  through theological narratives.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Scholasticism (Leff, Kretzmann, Luscombe): Show institutional embedding of binary disputation.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Logic (Aristotle, Boole, Peirce, Kneales): Show the formalization of binary reasoning.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Anthropology/Sociology (Goody, Douglas): Provide mechanisms of compression through literacy and taboo.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Together these sources substantiate the staged collapse  over ~1400 years, and the hysteresis that made Boolean algebra the inevitable closure.</p></div><section class="inline-media-container flex flex-col gap-1"></section><section class="auth-notification flex flex-col gap-1"></section></div><div class="order-first sticky hidden @md/mainview:block top-11 h-0 opacity-0 group-focus-within:opacity-100 group-hover:opacity-100 -mr-[36px] @lg/mainview:-mr-12"><div class="min-h-7 py-4 flex flex-col gap-0.5"><button class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-medium leading-[normal] cursor-pointer focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-1 focus-visible:ring-ring disabled:opacity-60 disabled:cursor-not-allowed transition-colors duration-100 [&amp;_svg]:shrink-0 select-none text-fg-secondary hover:text-fg-primary hover:bg-button-ghost-hover disabled:hover:text-fg-secondary disabled:hover:bg-transparent [&amp;_svg]:hover:text-fg-primary h-10 w-10 rounded-full" type="button" aria-label="Edit" data-state="closed"><span style="opacity: 1; transform: none;"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="stroke-[2] size-4"><path d="M18.25 5.75C16.8693 4.36929 14.6307 4.36929 13.25 5.75L10.125 8.875L5.52404 13.476C4.86236 14.1376 4.45361 15.0104 4.36889 15.9423L4 20.0001L8.0578 19.6311C8.98967 19.5464 9.86234 19.1377 10.524 18.476L18.25 10.75C19.6307 9.36929 19.6307 7.13071 18.25 5.75V5.75Z" stroke="currentColor"></path><path d="M12.5 7.5L16.5 11.5" stroke="currentColor"></path></svg></span></button><button class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-medium leading-[normal] cursor-pointer focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-1 focus-visible:ring-ring disabled:cursor-not-allowed transition-colors duration-100 [&amp;_svg]:shrink-0 select-none text-fg-secondary hover:text-f