</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(475.2px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(496.8px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\maketitle</span><span>
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(518.4px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(540px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\begin</span><span>{abstract}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(561.6px);"><span>We critique the catharsis model of emotionâ€”derived from Freudian hydraulics and still culturally pervasiveâ€”as theoretically incoherent. Rather than â€œreleasingâ€ pressure, indulging sadness or anger re-excites the same dynamical attractors, prolonging emotional dwell time. We propose a replacement grounded in Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP) field dynamics and Geometric Bayesianism with Sparse Heuristics (GBSH). Emotions are attractor basins in constrained inference geometry, shaped by metabolic sparsity and comparative reference frames. This framework explains why catharsis amplifies affect, why reframing alters valence without arousal escalation, and why personality â€œtraitsâ€ are environmental averages rather than fixed quantities.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(583.2px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\end</span><span>{abstract}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(604.8px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(626.4px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\section</span><span>{Introduction}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(648px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(669.6px);"><span>The concept of catharsis has long been embedded in psychological discourse and popular culture, originating from ancient Greek drama but gaining modern prominence through Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theories. In Freud's framework, emotions were likened to hydraulic pressures building within the psyche, necessitating release through expression or sublimation to prevent pathological buildup. This hydraulic metaphor persists today in therapeutic advice, self-help literature, and everyday language, where individuals are encouraged to "vent" frustrations or "let out" sadness to achieve emotional equilibrium. However, a growing body of empirical research challenges this view, indicating that such expressive behaviors often intensify rather than alleviate negative emotional states. For instance, studies on anger expression have shown that venting can lead to heightened aggression and prolonged arousal, suggesting a feedback mechanism at play rather than a simple discharge.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(691.2px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(712.8px);"><span>This discrepancy underscores the need for a more sophisticated, biologically plausible model of emotion that moves beyond static metaphors. In this paper, we advance the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP) field dynamics integrated with Geometric Bayesianism with Sparse Heuristics (GBSH) as a comprehensive alternative. RSVP conceptualizes cognition and emotion as emergent properties of interacting scalar, vector, and entropy fields, drawing from principles in physics and systems theory. GBSH extends this by incorporating geometric inference and sparsity constraints, aligning with Bayesian models of perception while accounting for metabolic and computational limitations in biological systems.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(734.4px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(756px);"><span>To fully appreciate this framework, some mathematical background is beneficial. Readers should be familiar with partial differential equations (PDEs) for field dynamics, Lyapunov stability for dissipative systems, and basic information geometry for manifold-based inference. Prerequisites include vector calculus (gradients, divergences, Laplacians) and optimization under constraints (e.g., Karush-Kuhn-Tucker conditions). We provide natural language explanations throughout to bridge formal derivations with intuitive understanding.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(777.6px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(799.2px);"><span>The paper proceeds as follows: Section 2 critiques catharsis in detail; Section 3 introduces RSVP with expanded derivations; Section 4 elaborates GBSH; Section 5 explores comparative affect; Section 6 outlines predictions; Section 7 discusses extensions to linguistic and logical domains; Section 8 examines practical implications; and Section 9 concludes.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(820.8px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(842.4px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\section</span><span>{Critique of Catharsis}
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(864px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(885.6px);"><span>The catharsis hypothesis posits that the overt expression of emotions serves as a purgative mechanism, releasing accumulated psychic energy and restoring psychological balance. This idea, traceable to Freud's early work on hysteria and the "talking cure," analogizes the mind to a hydraulic system where pressure (affect) builds and must be vented to avoid overflow. Culturally, this manifests in practices like "rage rooms" or expressive therapies, where physical or verbal outlets are presumed therapeutic. However, this model suffers from several conceptual flaws.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(907.2px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(928.8px);"><span>First, it assumes emotions are quantifiable substances akin to fluids, ignorning their dynamic, context-dependent nature. In reality, emotions arise from interactions between cognitive appraisals, physiological responses, and environmental cues, not as isolated pressures. Second, empirical studies reveal that cathartic behaviors often perpetuate affect through reinforcement loops; for example, ruminating on sadness can entrench negative thought patterns via attentional bias.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(950.4px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(972px);"><span>From an RSVP perspective, emotions are not reservoirs but attractor states in a dissipative field. "Venting" injects exogenous forcing into the system, akin to perturbing a stable orbit, which re-excites modes and extends basin dwell times. This positive feedbackâ€”modeled as energy injection along arousal directionsâ€”misinterprets amplification as release. Comparatively, catharsis is not entirely false but a misdiagnosis: it overlooks how expression entrains the very dynamics it seeks to dispel.
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(993.6px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(1015.2px);"><span>Mathematical background: Feedback systems in PDEs often exhibit bifurcations (e.g., Hopf) where small inputs lead to oscillatory regimes. Prerequisites include stability analysis and phase portraits.
